    "case_id": "73",
    "case_summary": "A 55-year-old man is brought to the emergency room by his wife for severe abdominal pain for the past 1 hour. He is unable to give more information about the nature of his pain. His wife says that he has peptic ulcer disease and is being treated with antacids without a good response. She adds that he vomited repeatedly  in the last couple of hours, his vomitus being brown/red in color. His temperature is 98.6 °F (37 °C), respiratory rate is 16/min, pulse is 97/min, and blood pressure is 100/68 mm Hg. A physical exam reveals a tense abdomen with a board like rigidity and positive rebound tenderness. An erect abdominal x-ray is ordered. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. perforated peptic ulcer\nB. gastrointestinal bleed\nC. pancreatitis\nD. bowel obstruction\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\n",
